            // ...
        }
    }
}--------------------
            Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```
We have also added some new variables, assertions and assumptions in these methods for verification.
The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is part of Pex's contract inference. It is used here to specify which method to analyze for the contract. 
The Assumption.IsTrue is a simple check to make sure the contract can be inferred correctly.
The PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used to display the values in the output for verification.
Finally, there are some Assertions that are there for verification, the last one is an assertion we want to use to verify our contract.
We are using PexContractInferred class method PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret); to show the return value in the output.
The test case is expected to fail, as it checks whether the LastIndexOf X from the original ArrayList is lesser than or equal to the IndexOf X in the new ArrayList. This is a logical fallacy as the LastIndexOf should always be greater than IndexOf if the element is in the list at all.



